Date: Thu, 23 Sep 93 2:38:10 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Crypto-Anarchy & Libertarian RealPolitik

Tim May encourages Extropian comments on social aspects of
crypto-anarchy, so I'll stir the pot a bit.  (I've also
added a postscript on Libertarian RealPolitik).

> On a Galt's Gulch sort of scenario, I think the "crypto anarchy"
> scenario I have been promoting is actually fairly likely to happen (it
> can be said to already be happening).

I'm a vocal supporter of crypto-anarchy, but must point out
there are severe problems with the disappear-into-cyberspace
scenario, including:

* Despite the hype about "information economy", the vast majority
of our world economy, and the most nonexpendable parts of it (eg
agriculture, goods distribution and housing) are still strongly bound 
to the physical world and vulnerable to coercion.   At some
expense one can make oneself or (more difficult) one's family somewhat 
"judgement proof", but this can be quite difficult for many people.

* There are many subtle problems with the "web of trust": the
need to trust central banks not to track digicash transactions,
the need to trust remailer operators not to log their traffic,
etc.  If we can't even motivate people to avoid using the POS-ATM
cards with which stores build a dossier of your every purchase, I 
fail to see how we can motivate trust in obscure mathematics,
which will be pure trust in authority for 99.9% of the users
and the vast majority of people owning or managing banks and
other financial enterprises.  A major gap in the crypto-anarchy
movement is lack of interest by the banking industry; cypherpunks
are almost entirely directed at political activism not commerce.
Banks would much rather build marketing dossiers for vendors
than protect the privacy of their customers, unless the customers
demand it, which they hardly ever do.

* The "competent people taking over the world" scenario is
silly.  Most of the people in control of the world are already
highly competent in the techniques needed to get to those
positions.  Many people highly competent in specific skills are
correspondingly incompetent other areas, especially political
awareness.  Allocating brain for one takes away brain for the 
other.  That's why people like Albert Einstein can be so brilliant 
in some spheres (physics) and ignorant babbling twits in others
(politics).  Furthermore, there is no rational self-interest
in having valuable political knowledge or opinions.  There
is no advantage, in terms of either money or personal satisfaction,
in being a libertarian instead of a socialist.  There are many
rich capitalists who are politically hard-core socialists.

Thus, since most "competent people" are either already in control,
not sympathetic to using control for libertarian purposes, or
both, most competent people won't participate in a "Galt strike".  

A more likely scenario is an emergent Galt strike: tax evasion 
on a massive scale motivated not by political ideals but by personal 
self-interest and change of habit as dodging the Information Highway 
Robbers becomes easy.  But such a phenomenon might also might be
self-limiting,  since after a point personal utility functions 
tend to favor the wide variety of human endevours that don't involve
making money (Tim May and perhaps even myself at this point are good 
examples).

Finally, the bureaucrats with Clipper, harassment of Phil
Zimmerman, etc. give every sign of being "on to us" and
aware of what crypto-anarchy would do to their power, and
they have massive power to institute things like Clipper
and Capstone, perhaps with an EC/NAFTA or even UN/Interpol
key escrow facility that eliminates the offshore escape
hatch.  The bureaucrats might well have the 
power to architect cyberspace to suit their needs.
In response there is layered encryption, steganography, etc.
but these are even more subtle and even more liable to being
pigeonholed as the tools only drug dealers and terrorists
would use.

postscript: Libertarian RealPolitik
-----------------------------------
The Libertarian Party vs. crypto-anarchy is
not an either/or issue.  We need many
talented hackers working on PGP interfaces, remailers,
digicash, on-line markets, etc.  We also need many bankers
and commercial lawyers setting up privacy-enhanced businesses.  
We also need a Libertarian Party and other efforts at education 
and political activism.  But I suggest these should be coordinated: 
the business practices can be done with libertarian politics in mind,
and the LP should put more emphasis on business practice
and technology as a potent sphere of political activism,
and less emphasis on electoral politics.  Furthermore
LP's lobbying can be more focused on those areas
that enable politically independent business: eliminate
crypto export restrictions and governmental key escrow schemes,
RKBA, school vouchers to allow independent education, etc.

Instead of an Oceania in need of defense let's put first things 
first and develop a voluntary libertarian militia and legal defense 
fund to defend our political supporters' property rights across 
jurisdictions.

What I'm suggesting here is a Libertarian RealPolitik
that combines the traditional LP with business interests and
economic privacy, led by a Libertarian Party that gets things done for 
its supporters in the real world instead of wasting so many 
contributions on futile attempts to win in the religious rituals of 
the ballot box.

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

