Perry Metzger writes:
>I've yet to be able to figure out how to handle milling the inside of
>the barrel properly. The tools to do this are normally quite
>specialized. I'm sure that you could build tools to do this without
>any trouble, but I have no idea how.

See "The Modern Riflebarrel: A complete manual for the making of rifle,
shotgun, and pistol barrels", by Harold Hoffman, 1988.  It gives
instructions for the whole process of barrel-making.  Disclaimer: I'm just
saying what the book says, I have no expertise in metalworking.

It describes a rifling method much faster and simpler than milling, called
button rifling.  This is done by pulling through the barrel a piece of
metal shaped so as to swage the rifling grooves.  The machine to do this
can be constructed from easily-obtainable hydraulic rams and thrust
bearings.  The most difficult part is making the rifling button, for which
a milling machine will be required, but I doubt if it would need one of the
5-axis multi-$k wonders mentioned here.  Once the rifling machine is made,
it takes about three minutes to rifle a barrel.

