From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: SPACE: Beanstalks and Bridges...

pgf asks about 'coilgun in orbit':

A good place to look is the entire series of conference reports
from the Princeton Conference on Space Manufacturing.  The individual
volumes are entitled "Space Manufacturing N", where N=1..8.  
Also do a search on Dr. Henry Kolm, who did a lot of the early
work.  He was a professor at MIT (he may still be).  There was
also, I believe, a periodic conference on electromagnetic accelerators,
which is reported in 'IEEE Transactions on Magnetics', one of their many
technical journals.  I may be a bit off in the last reference, but
it should be close.  You should be able to find all this stuff in
a good engineering library.

pgf asks 'why worry about project mass/payload mass?:

I use this ratio in two ways.  One is to show the scaling of various
structural systems.  The mass ratio is very sensitive to the velocity
you try to get out of a space bridge, but the mass ratio is independant
of the payload size.  Then I go and mutliply by typical structures
cost to get total project cost.  The second way to use this ratio is
to get an idea of how long it will take to build the system.  If what
you are building is the lowest cost transportation system, then you
should use yourself to deliver your components.  For example, use
a partially built space bridge to help lift cable to expand the
space bridge.  The mass ratio of the space bridge:payload (and the time
to handle a payload) govern how fast you can 'bootstrap'.

Dani Eder
eder@hsvaic.boeing.com

