Even if it was unintended, this list has been quite effective in 
spreading the meme to at least one person, me.  I read an ad for it
posted to alt.cyberpunks last year and have been hooked every since.  
This list has made me aware of cryonics and uploading as more than 
fringe science-fiction curiosities -- there are actually some
very well-clued-in, smart people exploring the workings and
the incredible consequences of these technologies, and in the
case of cryonics making them happen.  If uploading and cryonics had 
ever crossed my mind before, I had surely dismissed them as fringe 
whackoisms, out there somewhere in the same region as UFOs and and 
oiji boards.  

But I consider myself lucky.  I would never have obtained a
credible view of extropian pursuits from print media, or from 
current Usenet postings.  Despite being a bookstore junkie I have 
never yet seen a copy of Extropy or Cryonics in a bookstore.  Until 
now none of my freinds or co-workers were clued in about extropianism 
either, or if they were never mentioned it to me.

The word "extropian" rarely shows up on Usenet and when it does, 
it's usually in some brief prejorative, eg "it's an elitist
philosophy, I think my extropian housemates are rather egotistical" 
(the only response to a recent query, "what's an extropian?" on 
alt.extropians).  There are many people on Usenet sympathetic with the 
values of advancing technology and freedom, but not realizing the depth 
of thinking that has gone into it, the talent that has assembled 
around it, or the difference that can be made in their own prospects.

That's why I strongly support getting a presence on alt.extropy.
If there's no major objection I'll start posting some of my
old essays and other articles there, and taking on some "clueless
newbie" questions and comments, though I can't promise to rigorously 
address them all in the way Dave Krieger suggested.  (For example
haven't thought up a good response to the "egotistical and
elitist" bit, except for "sure, so what?" which satisfies me 
but probably not many casual Usenet readers and certainly not the 
flamers).

I suggest we _not_ give out the extropian list address to
alt.extropians unless we become satisfied that its readership
is largely sympathetic.  We can invite people that show great
interest in extropian stuff.  For example, I took the liberty of 
inviting a student who recently posted an excellent technical 
article on uploading, complete with references, on rec.arts.sf.science.

