Date: Thu, 23 Sep 93 10:00:42 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: SPACE: limits on launch costs

Building Large Towers

The first step in building a large tower is realizing it is possible.
Consider an off-the-shelf carbon fiber/epoxy composite (AMOCO Performance
Products T40 fiber and ERL-1906 epoxy to be specific).  It has a compressive
strength of 280,000 psi.  Assume we work at 60% of full strength.  This is
168,000 psi.  The density of this stuff is 0.058 lb/in^3.  Therefore, a
column 168,000/0.058 = 2,896,500 inches tall of this composite would be
at the maximum working stress at the bottom of the column.  In SI units,
this is 73.57 km tall.  If we have a 300 km tall tower with a payload
at the top, each km we go down from the top we must increase the structural
cross section to account for the additional weight of that km of structure.

In this case, the increase must be 1/73.57 per km, or 1.36% per km.  Over
300 km, the cross sectional area must increase (1.0136)^300 = 57 times.
If the payload at the top is 100 tons, then the tower structure will
have a cross section of 1.3 square inches at the top, and 75 square inches
at the bottom.  Given the density above, we can calculate the total
structural mass as 5740 tons, which can also be found by taking the
payload times the ratio of cross section at bottom and top.

Such a structure will not be a solid building.  Like power line
towers or radio masts, it will mostly be empty space with truss elements
spaced out to give it stability against buckling.  For stability, a
typical figure is a base 1/20 of the height across, so the base of
the tower will span 15 km.  It would be a triangular base, so there
would be 3 corners.  Each corner column itself would be a truss.  A rule
for designing not to buckle is a width of 1/20 the column height.  
So if the height to the first cross beam is 15 km, then the column
sub-columns need to be 750 meters apart.  There would be 9 of these.
In continuing subdivision of the tower trusses, the third order
columns would be 37.5 m apart, and the 4th order columns would
be 1.875 m apart.  There would be 81 of these, and each one would
have a cross sectional area of 0.925 square inches, which is a
manageable size to manufacture.  Each fourth order column supports a 
load of 72 tons, which is met by a concrete footing.  The entire
tower does not provide much shade, so the ground in between the
footings could be used for,say, agriculture.

For the first 20 km in height, wind loads are the dominant design
load.  To reduce wind loads dramatically, airfoil shaped fairings
are mounted on each strut on bearings so they can turn into
the wind.  THis reduces the drag coefficient from that of a round
rod (Cd=1) to below 0.05.  Still, to resist the remaining wind
forces, either guy wires or spreading the base of the tower for
the bottom 20 km is required, such as by adding diagonal bracing
struts on the bottom 20 km.

To actually build such a structure without a lot of space-suited
construction workers, you buy a set of hydraulic jacks and jack
up the tower in convenient increments, install some structure,
then repeat.  You acutally build the tower from the top down, so to
speak.  It might even be possible to continuously extrude the
columns by methods similar to those being used in the aerospace
industry to make large composite spars for aircraft, but makinjg
the struts in a factory and assembling them on-site like any
other building would work just as well.  

Dani Eder


